[al:新概念英语第四册]
[ti:22. Porpoises]
[00:00.240] Passage 22 | 文章 22
[00:02.030] There has long been a superstition among mariners that porpoises will save drowning men | 海员中长期以来流传着一种迷信，认为海豚会将溺水者救活，
[00:08.035] by pushing them to the surface, | 方式是把他们推到水面，
[00:10.150] or protect them from sharks by surrounding them in defensive formation. | 或者通过形成防御阵型包围鲨鱼来保护他们。
[00:15.445] Marine studio biologists have pointed out that, | 海洋工作室的生物学家指出，
[00:19.370] however intelligent they may be, | 无论它们多么聪明，
[00:21.690] it is probably a mistake to credit dolphins with any motive of life-saving. | 将任何救生动机归功于海豚可能都是一个错误。
[00:27.280] On the occasions when they have pushed ashore an unconscious human being, | 在它们将一个失去知觉的人推到岸边的时候，
[00:32.180] they have much more likely done it out of curiosity or for sport, | 它们很可能更多是出于好奇或为了玩耍而这么做的，
[00:36.595] as in riding the bow waves of a ship. | 就像骑着船头浪一样。
[00:38.690] In 1928, some porpoises were photographed working like beavers | 1928年，一些海豚被拍到像海狸一样工作，
[00:44.980] to push ashore a water-logged mattress. | 将一个浸水的床垫推上岸。
[00:48.030] If, as has been reported, | 如果，正如报道所说，
[00:51.135] they have protected humans from sharks, | 它们保护了人类免受鲨鱼的侵害，
[00:53.820] it may have been because curiosity attracted them, | 那可能是因为好奇心吸引了它们，
[00:57.345] and because the scent of a possible meal attracted the sharks. | 也可能是因为潜在食物的气味吸引了鲨鱼。
[01:01.775] Porpoises and sharks are natural enemies. | 海豚和鲨鱼是天敌。
[01:05.675] It is possible that upon such an occasion a battle ensued, | 很可能在这种情况下发生了一场战斗，
[01:10.035] with the sharks being driven away or killed. | 鲨鱼被赶走或杀死。
[01:13.855] Whether it be bird, fish or beast, | 无论是鸟、鱼还是野兽，
[01:17.650] the porpoise is intrigued with anything that is alive. | 海豚对任何活着的生物都很好奇。
[01:21.070] They are constantly after the turtles, the Ferdinands of marine life, | 它们总是追逐海龟，这些海洋生物中的“费迪南德”，
[01:26.195] who peacefully submit to all sorts of indignities. | 它们平静地忍受着各种侮辱。
[01:30.105] One young calf especially enjoyed raising a turtle to the surface with his snout, | 一只幼崽特别喜欢用它的吻将海龟推到水面，
[01:36.110] and then shoving him across the tank like an aquaplane. | 然后像水上飞机一样把它推过水箱。
[01:40.120] Almost any day a young porpoise may be seen | 几乎每天都能看到一只幼年海豚
[01:43.925] trying to turn a 300-pound sea turtle over | 试图翻转一只300磅重的海龟，
[01:47.240] by sticking his snout under the edge of his shell and pushing up for dear life. | 它把吻伸到海龟壳的边缘下面，拼命向上推。
[01:53.215] This is not easy, | 这并不容易，
[01:55.115] and may require two porpoises working together. | 可能需要两只海豚一起合作。
[01:58.545] In another game, as the turtle swims across the oceanarium, | 在另一个游戏中，当海龟游过海洋馆时，
[02:03.285] the first porpoise swoops down from above | 第一只海豚从上方俯冲下来，
[02:06.070] and butts his shell with his belly. | 用肚子撞击它的壳。
[02:08.885] This knocks the turtle down several feet. | 这会将海龟击落几英尺。
[02:12.335] He no sooner recovers his equilibrium | 它刚恢复平衡，
[02:15.030] than the next porpoise comes along and hits him another crack. | 下一只海豚就过来再撞它一下。
[02:18.985] Eventually, the turtle has been butted all the way down to the floor of the tank. | 最终，海龟被撞到了水箱底部。
[02:23.795] He is now satisfied merely to try to stand up, | 它现在仅仅满足于尝试站起来，
[02:27.165] but as soon as he does so, a porpoise knocks him flat. | 但它一这样做，一只海豚就把它撞平了。
[02:31.330] The turtle at last gives up, | 海龟最终放弃了，
[02:33.440] by pulling his feet under his shell, and the game is over. | 把脚缩进壳里，游戏结束。
[02:37.840] Ralph Nading Hill, | 拉尔夫·内丁·希尔，
[02:40.495] Window in the Sea. | 海中的窗户。